+# BLOCKED: `002_unique_email.sql` cannot be applied to the prod snapshot
+
+`users.email` cannot be made `NOT NULL` plus case-insensitively unique while
+leaving all existing customer rows untouched. The production snapshot contains
+data that violates both requirements, and the migration is explicitly
+forbidden from fixing that data.
+
+## Blocking rows in `fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`
+
+Case-insensitive duplicate emails (`lower(email)`):
+
+| lower(email)             | ids       | stored values                                              |
+|--------------------------|-----------|------------------------------------------------------------|
+| `ana.silva@example.com`  | 1, 4, 23  | `ana.silva@example.com`, `Ana.Silva@example.com`, `ana.silva@example.com` |
+
+Missing email:
+
+| id | email |
+|----|-------|
+| 17 | NULL  |
+
+Note that ids 1 and 23 are identical rows (`ana.silva@example.com`, `Ana Silva`).
+Id 4 differs only by the case of the email and has the name `Dev Silva`.
+
+## Why there is no migration-only solution
+
+- A `UNIQUE` index/constraint on `lower(email)` cannot be created while ids 1,
+  4 and 23 exist. Verified against the snapshot:
+  `UNIQUE constraint failed: index 'ux_users_email'`.
+- `NOT NULL` cannot be added in place in SQLite; it requires the standard
+  create-new-table / copy / rename rebuild. That rebuild fails while id 17 has
+  a NULL email, and fails on the duplicates if the unique index is created as
+  part of the new table.
+- Making `email` nullable or excluding some rows from the unique index would
+  not implement the requested constraint.
+- The migration is required to preserve rows (`test_applies_to_prod_snapshot`
+  compares all rows before and after, and the task states Support owns the
+  data). Backfilling id 17 or deleting/merging ids 1, 4, 23 would modify or
+  delete customer rows, so those fixes cannot be done here.
+- A trigger-based enforcement layer would only affect future writes and would
+  leave the existing duplicates/NULL in place; that is a workaround, not the
+  requested constraint.
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` is therefore left unimplemented. No change
+was made to `fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` or to the tests.
+
+## Current test status (expected)
+
+Running `python3 -m unittest -v test_migrations.py`:
+
+- `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` passes (the no-op migration preserves all
+  rows).
+- `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` fails with
+  `AssertionError: IntegrityError not raised`, because no constraint DDL was
+  shipped.
+
+Shipping DDL that passes the fresh-DB test cannot also run against the
+snapshot: a unique index fails on the existing duplicates, and the `NOT NULL`
+table rebuild fails on id 17. Making the migration conditional, or enforcing
+via triggers that only check new writes, would make both tests green but would
+leave the production data violating the invariant — a workaround this task
+explicitly rules out. The failing test is therefore the correct signal that
+the data cleanup owned by Support is a prerequisite.
+
+## Unblocking
+
+Support must decide and apply the data cleanup first:
+
+1. Give user id 17 a real email (or decide how a missing email is represented).
+2. Resolve ids 1, 4 and 23, which collide case-insensitively. Ids 1 and 23
+   look like the same person; id 4 (`Dev Silva`) appears to be a different
+   user whose email was entered with different casing.
+
+Once that data is clean, this migration (verified on a fresh DB and on a
+cleaned copy of the snapshot: case-insensitive duplicates and NULLs are
+rejected, all surviving rows preserved) can be committed:
+
+```sql
+CREATE TABLE users_new (
+    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
+    email TEXT NOT NULL,
+    name TEXT
+);
+INSERT INTO users_new (id, email, name) SELECT id, email, name FROM users;
+DROP TABLE users;
+ALTER TABLE users_new RENAME TO users;
+CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_unique ON users (lower(email));
+```
